<DocsLayout title="Introduction" description="Set up deterministic design-system guardrails for AI coding agents">
  <h1>Introduction to Buoy</h1>

  <p>
    Buoy is a deterministic design reviewer for AI-generated frontend code. The open-source CLI, MCP server, and GitHub App help Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot, and developers follow the tokens and component rules already in a repository.
  </p>

  <h2>The Missing Feedback Loop</h2>

  <p>
    In development, we have robust feedback mechanisms: tests catch bugs, linters enforce style,
    type checkers prevent errors. These tools give developers immediate signals when something is wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    Design systems have no equivalent. When AI generates <code>padding: 15px</code> instead of your
    16px token, nothing breaks. No test fails. No error appears. The code ships, and your design
    system slowly erodes.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Buoy is that missing feedback loop.</strong> The scanner applies explicit repository rules, so the same input produces the same findings without prompt or model variance. Optional AI explains those findings; it does not invent them.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Design Drift?</h2>

  <p>
    Design drift happens when code gradually diverges from your design system. It's the accumulation of small
    inconsistencies that add up over time:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A developer eyeballs a color and types <code>#3b83f6</code> instead of <code>#3b82f6</code></li>
    <li>AI generates a component with <code>padding: 15px</code> instead of your 16px spacing token</li>
    <li>Someone copy-pastes a button with hardcoded styles instead of using the design system component</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    After 6 months, you end up with 47 shades of "blue" and 12 different spacing values. Buoy catches this drift
    before it ships.
  </p>

  <h2>Quick Example</h2>

  <pre><code>$ npx ahoybuoy drift

🛟 Scanning for design drift...

Found 23 issues in 142 files

HARDCODED VALUES (8)
├─ Card.tsx:8 #ffffff → use var(--bg-surface)
├─ Badge.tsx:3 8px → use var(--spacing-sm)
└─ ... and 6 more

ACCESSIBILITY (2)
├─ Button.tsx:12 Missing aria-label
└─ Modal.tsx:45 Focus trap not implemented
</code></pre>

  <h2>Key Features</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero Config</strong> — Auto-detects React, Vue, Svelte, Angular, Next.js, Tailwind, and 50+ frameworks</li>
    <li><strong>Drift Detection</strong> — Finds hardcoded colors, spacing, typography, arbitrary Tailwind, inline styles with error/warning/info severity levels</li>
    <li><strong>Smart Fix Column</strong> — Shows &#x1F3AF; for exact token matches, &#x2248; for the nearest token when the value differs, and &#x1F4A1; for suggested new tokens, so you know what's safe to apply</li>
    <li><strong>Token Regressions</strong> — Flags a PR that replaces a token reference with its literal value, with a one-click fix that restores the token</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-Fix</strong> — Suggests and applies token replacements with <code>buoy drift fix --apply</code></li>
    <li><strong>Health Scoring</strong> — 0-100 score from a single source of truth (the scanner), showing your design system health via <code>buoy show health</code></li>
    <li><strong>Token Detection</strong> — Auto-detects tokens from CSS custom properties, JS theme objects, and Tailwind config</li>
    <li><strong>Coding Agents</strong> — An <a href="/docs/integrations/mcp">MCP server and Claude Code plugin</a> give agents your tokens while they write and check each edit for drift</li>
    <li><strong>Ignore Rules</strong> — Ignore existing drift from CLI or dashboard so you only see new issues</li>
  </ul>

